<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Townshend Acts</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1767">1767</a>–<a href="/years/1768">1768</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Townshend duties</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Townshend Acts were a series of laws passed by the British Parliament in 1767 and 1768, named after the chancellor of the exchequer Charles Townshend, that imposed duties on glass, lead, paint, paper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> imported into the American colonies. Colonial boycotts led to the repeal of all the duties except that on tea in 1770, which later provoked the Boston Tea Party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/townshend-acts.jpg" alt="Townshend Acts" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joshua Reynolds · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CharlesTownshend.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
